                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3817ecb2-357a-3bdc-b086-158faea2d1ec",
                        "entry_id": "5338ccb5-f278-396c-80d3-61f1d5415d35",
                        "glosses": [
                            "gewoon, alledaags"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "40e79431-c3af-41a0-b2b7-4df0f59bc102",
                                "sense_id": "3817ecb2-357a-3bdc-b086-158faea2d1ec",
                                "text": "Het leven is meer proza dan poëzie"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "14370414-d34b-3c2d-9a80-b2c766ee85d1",
                        "entry_id": "5338ccb5-f278-396c-80d3-61f1d5415d35",
                        "glosses": [
                            "gewone verhalende tekst zonder rijm of vormvastheid"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "377f80da-92f6-48b5-94c8-5ab0277782d1",
                                "sense_id": "14370414-d34b-3c2d-9a80-b2c766ee85d1",
                                "text": "Proza kent weinig regels qua vorm van de tekst."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4ca21ed7-4ff7-4297-93bd-9b1478b33b6d",
                                "sense_id": "14370414-d34b-3c2d-9a80-b2c766ee85d1",
                                "text": "In De andere naam van Jon Fosse is de stijl zeker eigen. De Noorse meester van het langzame proza daalt in repetitieve, ritmische zinnen af in het hoofd van een piekerende schilder."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "89f5cf62-a2a8-4347-aefe-135371522da2",
                        "source_entry_id": "5338ccb5-f278-396c-80d3-61f1d5415d35",
                        "target_lang_word": "prose",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "prose",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4efc92fe-228e-36b4-a14e-ab2b221ab35f",
                                        "entry_id": "16fa4300-25a6-367a-a8f3-a376aa3f2c06",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Language, particularly written language, not intended as poetry."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "209f1ad3-1c2e-47e6-9124-4df301ec8d43",
                                                "sense_id": "4efc92fe-228e-36b4-a14e-ab2b221ab35f",
                                                "text": "Though known mostly for her prose, she also produced a small body of excellent poems."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6ca0353e-e580-4a51-ba6c-ac9692e5642b",
                                                "sense_id": "4efc92fe-228e-36b4-a14e-ab2b221ab35f",
                                                "text": "...Or if Sion Hill\nDelight thee more, and Siloa's Brook that flow’d\nFaft by the Oracle of God; I thence\nInvoke thy aid to my adventrous Song,\nThat with no middle flight intends to soar\nAbove th’ Ionian Mounts while it pursues\nThings unattempted yet in Prose or Rhime..."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c947b1c5-f119-33e4-af0e-3906dd4cfdb9",
                                        "entry_id": "16fa4300-25a6-367a-a8f3-a376aa3f2c06",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Language which evinces little imagination or animation; dull and commonplace discourse."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "861d7a40-2a46-4f4d-ac3e-65a5b1e7fcd1",
                                                "sense_id": "c947b1c5-f119-33e4-af0e-3906dd4cfdb9",
                                                "text": "...the vehicle is plodding prose, but the effect is none the less poignant. And in regard to this I may say that in a hundred places in Trollope the extremity of pathos is reached by the homeliest means."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4e4d2835-ba29-3b26-861f-03f36a1e9063",
                                        "entry_id": "16fa4300-25a6-367a-a8f3-a376aa3f2c06",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A hymn with no regular meter, sometimes introduced into the Mass."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "41e97e3a-d693-433f-8694-159083e9d8fc",
                                                "sense_id": "4e4d2835-ba29-3b26-861f-03f36a1e9063",
                                                "text": "Proses are parts of the Office of the Mass which are sung just before the Gospel, upon great Festivals. The French also call those Rhythmical Hymns Proses, which are sung in their Offices in the Church of Rome, in which Rhime only, and not Quantity of Syllables, is observed."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "prose",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0df91e54-5c97-3ab2-b29c-100bfadec9f1",
                                        "entry_id": "6c1a1baa-2d7e-3e62-a511-21eb3b5c60e1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To write or repeat in a dull, tedious, or prosy way."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "07474058-5390-46f9-bcff-7f278b13255e",
                                                "sense_id": "0df91e54-5c97-3ab2-b29c-100bfadec9f1",
                                                "text": "Pray, do not prose, good Ethelbert, but speak;\nWhat is your purpose?"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "83e315ec-76a2-4651-883f-c1dd55140be2",
                                                "sense_id": "0df91e54-5c97-3ab2-b29c-100bfadec9f1",
                                                "text": "Already he felt himself near to being a celebrity. He had astonished Eton. That was a good beginning. Papa might prose, knowing, of course, nothing of the poetry of caricature, of the wild joys and the laurels that crown the whimsical. So while Mr. Lane hunted adjectives, and ran sad-sounding and damnatory substantives to earth, Eustace hugged himself, and secretly chuckled over his pilgrim's progress towards the pages of Vanity Fair."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "11c8b053-f3be-4bdc-831f-587370378171",
                        "source_entry_id": "5338ccb5-f278-396c-80d3-61f1d5415d35",
                        "target_lang_word": "prosa",
                        "target_lang_code": "es",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "prosa",
                                "target_lang_code": "es",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8dd00619-849c-327b-bd11-6afa3b4a082d",
                                        "entry_id": "7810d4ae-7610-380f-a35c-264acfe6d5dc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Forma textual que, en oposición al verso, no está regida explícitamente por cánones métricos y rítmicos."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a5c8df9d-cf20-4784-b58f-ddf936a5a309",
                                                "sense_id": "8dd00619-849c-327b-bd11-6afa3b4a082d",
                                                "text": "—Filósofo: ¿Y son versos los que queréis escribirle?\n—M. Jourdain: No, no; nada de versos.\n—F:¿Preferís la prosa?\n—J:No. No quiero ni verso ni prosa.\n—F:¡Pues una cosa u otra ha de ser!\n—J:¿Por qué?\n—F:Por la sencilla razón, señor mío, de que no hay más que dos maneras de expresarse: en prosa o en verso.\n—J:¿Conque no hay más que prosa o verso?\n—F:Nada más. Y todo lo que no está en prosa está en verso; y todo lo que no está en verso, está en prosa.\n—J:Y cuando uno habla, ¿en qué habla?\n—F:En prosa.\n—J:¡Cómo! Cuando yo le digo a Nicolasa: \"Tráeme las zapatillas\" o \"dame el gorro de dormir\", ¿hablo en prosa?\n—F:Sí, señor.\n—J:¡Por vida de Dios! ¡Más de cuarenta años que hablo en prosa sin saberlo! No sé cómo pagaros esta lección."
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "44eac80e-5c13-3f76-846e-4d4234d39227",
                                        "entry_id": "7810d4ae-7610-380f-a35c-264acfe6d5dc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Lenguaje que se recita o enuncia sin artificios canoros o vocales, especialmente en una circunstancia o ceremonia en que predomina la forma cantada."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"2\""
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b1e08be6-88aa-3f39-886f-03e4de0ba5a0",
                                        "entry_id": "7810d4ae-7610-380f-a35c-264acfe6d5dc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Parte más vulgar o coloquial de algo."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"3\""
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9dbba059-6681-3f47-b503-7f8ad389c276",
                                        "entry_id": "7810d4ae-7610-380f-a35c-264acfe6d5dc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Espaciado que se deja horizontalmente entre dos caracteres en la composición, y que en la mayoría de los tipos varía de acuerdo a la naturaleza y forma de los caracteres adyacentes. En la tipografía propia de las máquinas de escribir o de las terminales de computadora la prosa es igual sin importar el ancho del caracter, llamándose monoespaciado."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
